Own RFS for real, speak on the channels where the AP is the requester

The kernel driver no longer contains an RFS server: its S5XXX_CH_RFS arm
hands every frame to the RFS port, because the two things the protocol
exists for both belong here. The CP writes its NV back over this channel,
and a driver cannot write files -- the in-kernel server could only ack
those writes and drop them, leaving the CP believing it had persisted
state that does not exist. So correct this daemon's banner, which
announced "standing by ... the kernel driver answers RFS": that is the
false claim its own comment says cost a day of debugging, and
rfs-standby-and-reroot.patch is where it came from.

Re-root the CP's opens on the carrierconfig/ component rather than a
fixed prefix. The plugin advertises downstream's
/vendor/firmware/carrierconfig in CARRIER_CONFIG_MANIFEST so the frame
stays byte-identical to the vendor RIL's, and this daemon is then the
only thing that has to know where the files really are. Keying on the
component means it does not matter which directory was advertised.

Add a prologue hook to the channel handlers, for the channels where the
AP is the requester rather than the responder. Without one such a channel
only ever drains the CP's unsolicited indications, which is what this
daemon used to do on every one of them: stock pushes 47 requests on
radioext between registration and its first IMS PDN and we sent none of
them. Those, and the nine on the modem-ML channel (0x83), are replayed
from the capture in android_replay.rs because the payloads are not
understood; the CP answers each with the sequence number it was given, so
the order is part of the data. The new oem_ml handler serves 0x83, which
the kernel driver now exposes along with every other channel the vendor
driver opens.

Thread two fields that were being sent as zero. SET_DATA_PROFILE's
bearer_bitmask at @0xee declared every profile valid on no radio
technology at all; it is the carrier database's `bearers` column shifted
left by one, because the database holds Android's framework encoding
(bit n-1) and the wire wants bit n -- Telus 681732 -> 1363464, Fizz
663552 -> 1327104, both exactly what this modem's own Android sends. And
the per-stack attach builder now takes the P-CSCF request at @0xdb
instead of hardcoding none, so a caller that creates the IMS context with
the attach itself can ask for the addresses there, which is the only
place the network will still grant them.

The in-crate builder tests are updated for both new parameters; 117 pass.

